# Development Preferences

These are personal defaults, not a mandate to rewrite existing code.
Repository-level instructions, public API compatibility, established
conventions, and configured formatters take precedence.

## C# Style

- Prefer clear value transformations. Use LINQ and lambdas when they are easier
  to read than straightforward control flow and are not materially less
  efficient.
- Keep each method focused on one responsibility and one level of abstraction.
  Extract low-level mechanics when they obscure the method's main operation.
- Use `var` when the type is obvious.
- For new internal APIs, do not add the `Async` suffix to asynchronous methods.
  Preserve an existing repository or public API naming convention.
- Use `ConfigureAwait(false)` only in reusable class libraries where caller
  context should not be captured. It is normally unnecessary in ASP.NET Core
  applications and worker services.
- Read current time through an injected `TimeProvider`, not
  `DateTime.UtcNow` or `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`. Pass the resulting value into
  pure domain logic. In tests that need time control, use `FakeTimeProvider`
  from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`.
- Prefer one primary top-level type per file. Nested types may remain with their
  owner when they are discriminated-union cases or moving them would require
  making the owner `partial`.

## Architecture and Errors

- For code with meaningful business rules, prefer a functional core and
  imperative shell: deterministic, side-effect-free domain operations
  surrounded by thin use-case coordination and I/O adapters. Use pure static
  functions where natural.
- Keep side effects and dependency wiring at system boundaries. Do not share
  mutable state across otherwise independent operations.
- Model expected domain outcomes, such as validation failures, missing values,
  and business-rule rejections, with the repository's established result or
  discriminated-union convention. Do not introduce a new result abstraction
  solely to comply with this preference.
- Use exceptions for programming errors and unexpected infrastructure failures.
  Translate or report them at the appropriate application boundary.

## Complexity and API Design

- Follow repository formatting for line length; otherwise aim for approximately
  80 columns. Treat 24 lines and cyclomatic complexity of 7 per method as review
  signals, not targets to satisfy mechanically.
- When a method has too many parameters, locals, or dependencies, split it by
  cohesive behavior. Introduce a parameter object only when the grouped values
  form a meaningful concept.
- Normalize only documented, harmless input variations at a boundary; reject
  ambiguous input. Parse into domain types that enforce their invariants, and
  avoid separate `IsValid` checks when invalid states can be unrepresentable.
- Use the X-out test as a heuristic for important domain APIs: mentally replace
  a method name with `X`. If that exposes excessive reliance on primitive or
  overly generic types, consider improving the API shape. Method names may
  still carry essential domain meaning.
- Write range comparisons in number-line order: `2 < x && x <= 5`.
- Avoid calling overridable members from base-class algorithms; doing so creates
  an implicit Template Method extension point. Prefer private or non-overridable
  helpers unless the extension point is intentional and documented.

## Testing

- Prefer fakes to mocks; use mocks when a fake would be impractical.
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert without comments that merely label those sections.
- Keep one behavior, and therefore one primary reason to fail, per test.
- For new behavior and bug fixes, observe the relevant test fail before the
  implementation when practical. Deliberately mutate working code only when
  test sensitivity is uncertain, and revert the mutation immediately.

## Diagnostics

- Log side effects and boundary outcomes; do not add logging to the pure core.
- Keep analyzer suppressions narrow and give each a concrete justification.

## .NET Tooling

When these `mise` tasks are available:

- Build: `mise run dotnet:build`
- Smoke tests (default): `mise run dotnet:test:smoke`
- Unit tests, excluding integration tests: `mise run dotnet:test`
- All tests, including integration tests: `mise run dotnet:test:full`
- Choose the narrowest adequate test tier. If a broader tier is already needed,
  do not run a narrower tier first. The test tasks build selected projects
  internally, so do not run a separate build first.
- After changing NuGet dependencies, run `mise run dotnet:build` before the
  smoke or unit tier because those tiers use a no-restore build. Use
  `mise run dotnet:restore` only when the lock file already matches the project
  dependencies; the restore task runs in locked mode.
- Run one test with:
  `dotnet test <project> --filter FullyQualifiedName~Pattern --verbosity minimal --consoleLoggerParameters:ErrorsOnly`
  Do not add `--no-build` after changing production or test code.

## Dotfiles Managed by chezmoi

- Paths such as `~/.claude`, `~/.codex`, `~/.gemini`, `~/.config`, and
  top-level dotfiles in `$HOME` may be deployed copies. Before editing one, run
  `chezmoi source-path <file>`. If it resolves to a managed source, edit that
  source rather than the deployed copy.
- Applying, committing, and pushing are separate actions. Apply only targets
  whose live configuration is in scope, then verify the rendered output. Do not
  commit or push without an explicit request.
- `.chezmoitemplates/agent-instructions` is a named template, not a deployed
  file. After editing it, apply
  `~/.config/ai-instructions/INSTRUCTIONS.md` and any agent-specific generated
  instruction files. Codex, Gemini, and Command Code read the shared file
  through symlinks and need no separate apply.
